course: dictionary
lesson: 7429
title: Politics: deciding together
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Politics is how groups of people make decisions and share things out when they cannot all have what they want. «Politics is how groups of people make decisions and share things out when they cannot all have what they want.» The key idea is deciding together.
explain: Politics can be big, like how a country is run through elections and laws, or small, like «playground politics», when friends in a group argue and take sides. The word beside it gives the clue: playground tells you where it happens, and taking sides tells you what it is.
explain: Your politics are your opinions about how the country should be run. «Grown-ups get angry about politics because it decides money, safety and fairness, and they value these differently.» You can disagree strongly and still be kind.
explain: To check a meaning, swap it into the sentence. «The class had to share the last ten crayons, so they used politics to decide who got which colour.» Does it still make sense? Yes, because politics means making decisions together when people want different things.
fact: Politics means the activities of governments and people who run a country, including elections and making laws.
fact: Politics can also mean arguing and plotting for power in any group, such as «office politics» or «playground politics».
fact: Politics is also the study of how countries are governed.
fact: Politics is an uncountable noun, so you say «some politics» or «a lot of politics», not «a politics».
fact: Useful phrases: go into politics, talk politics, play politics.
exercise: «Politics is how groups of people make decisions and share things out when they cannot all have what they want.» What is politics? | Playing hockey on a frozen lake | *Making decisions and sharing things out when people cannot all have what they want | A type of cooking that uses many pans | Only the study of old castles
exercise: What does «playground politics» mean? | A new game with rules | Playing football at recess | *Friends in a group arguing and taking sides | Lessons about voting in school
exercise: What do a person's politics mean? | *Their opinions about how the country should be run | The way they walk to school | Their favourite sports team | Their pocket money
test: Which phrase means arguing and taking sides in a friendship group? | Go into politics | *Playground politics | Watch the news | Talk politics
test: Some nurses have gone on strike to ask for more pay. Why does this feel like politics to them? | *Because a decision that touches their own job and pay is political | Because they like hospitals | Because strikes always happen at the seaside | Because nurses wear uniforms at work
exam: Which meaning of politics fits best in a citizenship lesson? | Cooking dinner for a big family | *The activities of governments and people who run a country, including elections and making laws | Games played at recess | A type of music played at weddings
